.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1221 PM PST Thu Nov 26 2026
(This evening through Friday night)

After a foggy start to the day for portions of the Bay Area and
Central Coast, fog cleared mid-morning giving way to sunshine and
high clouds. Not a bad way to kickoff a holiday. Temperatures this
afternoon will top out in the 60s to lower 70s, which is a few
degrees above normal for late November. Curious weather watchers
may even notice a few weather stations along the immediate coast
reporting mild temperatures despite being right on the water.
Half Moon Bay for instance is currently sitting in the upper 60s.
Why? All about the wind direction. NE flow for that location is a
downslope flow regime. Air descending off the higher terrain to
the east compresses and then eventually warms.

Rest of the short term - we`re going to briefly close the storm
door. Upper level ridging built over the region the last 12 hours.
The upper level jet, or conveyor belt for our recent active
weather, has shifted farther north as well. As a result, we`ll be
high and dry through Friday night. We`ll continue to have some
high clouds as moisture ridges over the ridge. Nighttime will
feature fog, especially the Central Valley. We`ll have to watch
the N Bay closely as they may need another dense fog advisory
early Friday morning. Friday should be a spectacular day
weatherwise as temperatures warm a few more degrees. If you`re out
enjoying the nice weather and decide to hit the coast use
caution. We still have coastal flooding concerns and a dangerous
surf zone due to large swell and waves.

