.LONG TERM...
Issued at 121 PM PST Thu Nov 26 2026
(Saturday through next Wednesday)

The large upper ridge of high pressure gets squeezed on Saturday
as the next storm approaches from the west. Saturday will be the
last dry day of the next seven days. Highs will remain continue to
be above normal with a few more 70s across the interior. While
above normal, pretty far from records as previous Novembers have
had some mild temps.

Saturday night into Sunday - we`ll be opening the storm door
again. Taking a look at the broader view across the Pacific one
can currently see a disturbance sitting just north of Hawaii. This
feature is our next rain maker. The squeezed ridge from Saturday
shifts east by Saturday night. While the core of the low will pass
to the north its associated frontal structure will slide through
the region. The warm sector will spread light to moderate rain
north to south Saturday night into early Sunday. The stronger
cold front then pushes through Sunday into Sunday night with
moderate to brief periods of heavy rain. This could be problematic
for those traveling home after the holiday weekend. Aside from
travel problems this system looks to be more beneficial when it
comes to rain.The one wrinkle is a little instability with the
fropa. Guidance continues to advertise a small chance (10-15%)
for thunderstorms across the N Bay. The passing cold front will
also cause an uptick in winds on Sunday, but not strong enough for
a wind advisory.

Longer range forecast remains more focused on storm system number
two slated to arrive Monday and through midweek. Unlike the system
one, which took a northern track, this system looks to be colder
and farther south toward Point Conception. Ensemble guidance
actually has decent agreement on this track with cluster analysis
showing a majority of the members on the southern track too.
Given the colder set up and cyclonic jet support one should expect
more rain and stronger winds. Speaking of winds, as noted by the
last discussion, we`ll see some strong wind develop over the
waters late Monday/Tuesday and possibly along the coast. We`ll be
watching closely as future Wind Advisories may be needed. As for
rain, this system will have a little more juice, but the bigger
tap back to Hawaii will be over SoCal. Regardless, rainfall looks
to be highest over the Central Coast. If it pans out, we could
see impacts to the Timber and Plaskett burn areas again. Still
expecting rain over the Bay Area, but less. A lot of the rainfall
forecast does focus on the track on this system so there is some
uncertainty too. Thunderstorms return to the forecast with
15-20%.

A brief break in the action midweek, but the Pacific remains
active with additional system headed toward CA late next week.

