.BEACHES...
Issued at 1216 AM PST Thu Nov 26 2026

Coastal flooding will be a major impact this week. The
combination of king tides, storm surge, and thermal expansion from
warm El Nino waters will bring the tide to near all-time record
territory.

A Coastal Flood Warning is in effect for bayshore locations along
the San Francisco and San Pablo Bays through 4 PM Thursday. A
Coastal Flood Advisory is in effect until 4 PM Thursday for the
Pacific Coast. These predictions are approaching the highest tide
ever recorded. At the San Francisco tidal gauge, high tide is
expected to be 2.2 ft above normal (8.0 ft MLLW) at 11:38 AM
Thursday, 2.0 ft above normal (7.8 ft MLLW) at 12:31 PM Friday,
and 2.0 ft above normal (7.8 ft MLLW) at 1:28 PM Saturday. These
predictions include up to 1.5 ft of surge that will enhance the
astronomical tide and flooding threat. High tide varies up to 90
minutes earlier or later along the Pacific Coast and through the
San Francisco Bay, respectively.

A Beach Hazards Statement remains in effect through 10 PM Friday
due to increased risk of sneaker waves, strong rip currents, and
breaking waves between 13 to 19 feet. These conditions will
coincide with King Tides and up to 1.5 ft of surge, bringing
water and waves farther up the beach than normal, restricting
escape from beaches and coastal areas. Large breaking waves can
overpower swimmers, pulling people into the sea from beaches,
jetties, and rocks resulting in significant physical injury and
increasing the risk of drowning. Inexperienced swimmers should
remain out of the water. Stay off of jetties, piers, and other
waterside infrastructure and never turn your back on the ocean!
Hazardous beach conditions will linger through the weekend with
additional rounds of hazardous conditions expected next week as
the storm door remains open.

